title: Sync
kind: overview
aliases: synchronise synchronize synchronisation synchronization cross-repo
summary: Two repositories loaded in one daemon kept in step: records linked across them, their metadata and files compared and copied, in two steps — a plan you can review, then a run.

Sync keeps a selection of two repositories in step: a record of one is ''linked''
to a record of the other, and each sync compares the two and carries what changed
on one side to the other — fields, positions, file contents, modes, deletions. What
is linked is a query of your choosing, per repository ([[The intents file]]); a
file is linked to the file at the same path on the other side ([[Matching records across repositories]]).

It works in two steps. `mf sync plan` reads both repositories and writes what it
would do into a ''plan'' — a repository of its own you can read and edit before
anything happens ([[The sync plan]]); `mf sync run` does it ([[Running a sync]]).
Both repositories must be loaded in the same daemon, with identical schemas
([[Why schemas must be identical]]).

Nothing is destroyed. A file a sync overwrites or removes goes to that
repository's trash ([[Trash]]); a metarecord it deletes is a revision of the event
log, which an undo takes back ([[Event log]]). What sync knows about a pair — its
links, and what the two sides held at their last sync — is kept apart from both
repositories' data ([[The sync database]]).

The orchestration runs in the clients, `mf sync` and the [[sync panel]], which share
one implementation (`core::sync`); the daemon offers the primitives it needs
([[Sync endpoints]]).

